at this point there is over 50 years  worth of Doctor Who where do you start  as someone who very obviously really  loves Doctor Who a question I will  sometimes get from people who know me  and have heard great things about the  show but I've never watched it before is  well where do I begin and that's really  a kind of a tough question to answer but  I'll start with what you don't do what  you don't do is go back to the first  episodes from 1963 and try and start  there there's a number of reasons you  don't do that aside from the fact that  it would take forever the big thing is  though I really don't think the classic  error is where any modern audience  should be starting you can enjoy the  classic era certainly but I think you're  gonna be better off starting with the  modern era get hooked into it first come  to like the character like the world and  then go back watch the older stuff the  older stuff it's very of its time it's  very dated in a lot of ways and it's  very held back by the budgetary  restrictions of the time there's still  some fantastic stuff from the classic  era but I don't think it's a good hook  or a good starting place for people  trying to get into this for the first  time again once you're into the show by  all means go back and watch it don't  feel obligated to watch classic era  stuff in chronological order though  because they're pretty much all  self-contained stories you can watch  them in any order you feel like but you  really you need to start somewhere on  the modern era which started in 2005  with Christopher Eccleston as the ninth  doctor now logically wouldn't you just  say so you start with the 2005 so with  series 1 then right no I'm not sure I'd  recommend that either the thing is is  that that first series I don't really  feel is particularly good representation  of the show at this point there was a  lot of stuff that went on in that first  series that got dropped a lot of goofy  aspects a lot of sort of more juvenile  more child as touches that got dropped  and really almost immediately by the  next series they've gotten rid of a lot  of that stuff so I'm not sure that  that's the best place to start either  and the other thing is even if you start  that that's still over ten years of  stuff that you've got to catch up on  from that which that can be a bit of a  chore so here's what I tend to recommend  to people thinking about getting into  Doctor Who for the first time my  recommendations are twofold I have a  test  episode and then I actually have a place  where I recommend that they start my  test episode is blink I will tell people  okay you've never seen Doctor Who go see  the episode blink just that episode  because I think blink is the episode  where you can go in knowing nothing  about the character nothing about the  premise nothing about anything you can  watch it you can follow that story you  can get what's going on you can get a  feel of the character you can get a feel  of the type of stories that get told you  can get a feel of what people love about  this show now if you watch blink and it  doesn't grab you and you're not left  going I want to see more stuff like this  you know what just walk away the shows  not for you and that's fine but if it  does intrigued you my own recommendation  for where to start after that is with  the eleventh hour which is Matt Smith's  first episode now I'm sure some people  who follow this channel are gonna be  saying well you're just saying that  because Matt Smith's your favorite  doctor and I'm not gonna say that's not  part of the reason I'm sure it probably  is but it's an option I kinda came to  really by process of elimination as I  said the start of series one the it's  just not indicative of the rest of the  show now maybe starting series two with  David Tennant that's an option I the  thing is though Christmas invasion which  this is first episode still it has a  little bit of the lingering goofiness  from series one and then the actual  proper start of series two with new  earth and then tooth and claw and some  other stuff it's there's a lot of stuff  I love in series two the way that it  starts isn't great and there's some  really rough episodes in that season  that's the season that I had fear her  and love and monsters so I'm not sure  that that's a series to recommend people  start with if you're trying to get them  to fall in love with the show starting  in series three receivers four could  work but by that point we're getting too  many callbacks on references to stuff  that had already gone on the show and  people are gonna know that they're  missing out on stuff now are there  callbacks at the start of Matt Smith's  era which was series 5 yes there are but  really that first episode especially the  11th hour really was designed as a  jumping on point and you get the doctor  and you get a new companion you get a  summary of how he works how the TARDIS  works how his life works and when Steven  Moffat who became the showrunner at the  start of that series took over he didn't  I'm not gonna say he didn't call back to  the stuff that had gone on before them  but it was more referential it didn't  dig deeply into what had gone on before  so the start of series 5 really does  work as AI has a very good starting  point now I suppose the other  possibility to consider would be deep  breath the start of series 8 which was  Peter Capaldi who at the time of this  recording is the current doctor but  again I don't feel that that episode  works particularly well as a jumping on  point the other thing as much as I  personally loved Capaldi's doctor he's a  big bristle ease a bit prickly  especially in his first series and I'm  not sure that he's gonna be as a pible  and as good at pulling in new people as  Matt Smith was who was just lovable and  warm and I I think he works a lot better  in terms of hooking people and again yes  I will admit he is my favorite doctor  but you have to admit the guy's just  kind of more welcoming just as a persona  as a personality so anyway that's my own  recommendation for what I tell people  where do you start  with Doctor Who now if you're a Doctor  Who fan you've gone all the way through  this video what starting point would you  recommend for people trying to get into  this show for the first time whatever  your thoughts are drop something down in  the comments and let's talk about it  please feel free to like subscribe check  out the council of geeks podcast  available on iTunes and stitcher and  until next time this council is  adjourned
 

